  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">11.5. Self-Check Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the interval of convergence of the power series $$\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n}}{n^{2} + 1}$$ . (Disregard the endpoints.)</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>We first find the radius of convergence of the power series.</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Since $$a_{n} = \frac{1}{n^{2} + 1}$$ , we have</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {R = {\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left| \frac{a_{n}}{a_{n + 1}} \right| = \lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\frac{1}{n^{2} + 1}}{\frac{1}{(n + 1)^{2} + 1}}}} \\ {\phantom{R} = {\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{(n + 1)^{2} + 1}{n^{2} + 1}}} \\ {\phantom{R} = {\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\left( {1 + \frac{1}{n}} \right)^{2} + \frac{1}{n^{2}}}{1 + \frac{1}{n^{2}}}} {{Dividing numerator and denominator by}n^{2}}} \\ {\phantom{R} = 1} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Therefore, the interval of convergence of the series is $$( - 1,1)$$ .</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the Taylor series of the function $$f(x) = e^{- x}$$ at $$x = 1$$ , and determine its interval of convergence.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Here, $$a = 1$$ and</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{rccrcc} {f(x)} &amp; = &amp; e^{- x} &amp; {f(1)} &amp; = &amp; e^{- 1} \\ {f^{\prime}(x)} &amp; = &amp; {- e^{- x}} &amp; {f^{\prime}(1)} &amp; = &amp; {- e^{- 1}} \\ {f^{''}(x)} &amp; = &amp; e^{- x} &amp; {f^{''}(1)} &amp; = &amp; e^{- 1} \\ {f^{'''}(x)} &amp; = &amp; {- e^{- x}} &amp; {f^{'''}(1)} &amp; = &amp; {- e^{- 1}} \\ &amp; \vdots &amp; &amp; &amp; \vdots &amp; \\ {f^{(n)}(x)} &amp; = &amp; {( - 1)^{n}e^{- x}} &amp; { f^{(n)}(1)} &amp; = &amp; {( - 1)^{n}e^{- 1}} \\ &amp; \vdots &amp; &amp; &amp; \vdots &amp; \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Therefore, the required Taylor series is</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {e^{- x} = {f(1) + f^{\prime}(1)(x - 1) + \frac{f^{''}(1)}{2!}(x - 1)^{2} + \frac{f^{'''}(1)}{3!}(x - 1)^{3} + \cdots + \frac{f^{(n)}(1)}{n!}(x - 1)^{n} + \cdots}} \\ {\phantom{e^{- x}} = {e^{- 1} - \frac{e^{- 1}}{1!}(x - 1) + \frac{e^{- 1}}{2!}(x - 1)^{2} - \frac{e^{- 1}}{3!}(x - 1)^{3} + \cdots + \frac{( - 1)^{n}e^{- 1}}{n!}(x - 1)^{n} + \cdots}} \\ {\phantom{e^{- x}} = {\frac{1}{e} - \frac{1}{e}(x - 1) + \frac{1}{2!e}(x - 1)^{2} - \frac{1}{3!e}(x - 1)^{3} + \cdots + \frac{( - 1)^{n}}{n!e}(x - 1)^{n} + \cdots}} \\ {\phantom{e^{- x}} = {\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{\infty}{\frac{( - 1)^{n}}{n!e}(x - 1)^{n}}}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>The interval of convergence is $$( - \infty,\infty)$$ . (See Example 5.)</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
